+## FAQ
+
+### What are the best open source AI tools for knowledge management in 2026?
+For general self-hosted usage, start with `AnythingLLM`, `Open WebUI`, `Flowise`, `RAGFlow`, and `Quivr`. If you need note-centric personal knowledge management, look at `Khoj`, `Reor`, `AFFiNE`, and `AppFlowy`.
+
+### Which GitHub repos are best for self-hosted AI knowledge bases?
+The most practical self-hosted options in this list are `AnythingLLM`, `MaxKB`, `DocsGPT`, `RAGFlow`, `Quivr`, and `private-gpt`. They combine or support ingestion, retrieval, and grounded chat over private documents.
+
+### Are there fully open source alternatives to proprietary AI knowledge management SaaS?
+Yes. Every project listed here has a public GitHub repository and an open source or source-available codebase you can inspect directly. This list excludes proprietary SaaS products that do not expose a public GitHub repository.
+
+### What is the difference between a RAG tool and a knowledge management tool?
+A RAG tool focuses on retrieval and grounded generation, while a knowledge management tool also includes organization, workflows, note systems, curation, access patterns, and reuse over time. In practice, the open source market still overlaps heavily, so many of the best AI tools for knowledge management are RAG-first projects.
+
+### Which open source AI repos work well for personal knowledge management?
+`Reor`, `Khoj`, `AFFiNE`, `AppFlowy`, and `SiYuan` are the strongest fits here. They help with notes, local-first storage, semantic retrieval, and building a searchable second brain around your own information.
+
+### Which open source AI tools are best for enterprise document search?
+`RAGFlow`, `Haystack`, `Docling`, `Unstructured`, `MaxKB`, and `Quivr` are strong choices when enterprise document complexity, parsing quality, and retrieval control matter more than a simple chat interface.
